{
  "configuration_id": "config_041",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_009 (multiprofile, scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted) achieved 0.706 accuracy, far outperforming the singleprofile baseline (0.537), confirming that multiprofile aggregation is critical. The current best uses avgpool, a simple uniform-weight aggregation that treats all profiles equally. Question: config_041 isolates the aggregation method, replacing avgpool with a transformer that can learn attention weights across profiles, while keeping all other settings (scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, weighted fusion, multiprofile) identical to the best result. This is a single-variable change, making the result easy to interpret. Rationale: Transformer aggregation is the most natural and impactful architectural upgrade from avgpool for multiprofile settings, as it can learn which profiles carry more signal. With 7 experiments remaining, this high-information experiment either confirms avgpool is sufficient (freeing us to explore encoders/fusion) or unlocks a new best result to build on. Expected cost: transformer aggregation adds self-attention over profiles, so I estimate ~6–7 GPU hours (moderately higher than config_009's 4.82), bringing the running total to roughly 11–12 GPU hours — acceptable given the strong expected accuracy upside."
}

